==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[maihem]]

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|mayme|lang=enm}}, {{term|mahaime|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|mahaim||mutilation|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|mahaign||bodily harm, loss of limb|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|gem|-}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*maidijaną||to cripple, injure}} (compare {{etyl|gmh|-}} ''meidem, meiden'' 'gelding', {{etyl|non|-}} ''meiða'' 'to injure', [[Gothic]] [[𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽]] ''maidjan'' 'to alter, falsify'),<ref>Philip Babcock, ed., ''Webster’s Third New International Dictionary of the English Language'', Unabridged, s.v. "mayhem" (Springfield, Mass: Merriam-Webster, Inc., 1993.</ref> from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*mei||to change}}. More at [[mad]]. The original meaning referred to the crime of maiming, the other senses derived from this.

Meaning #1 may have arisen by popular misunderstanding of the common [[journalese]] expression "[[rioting]] and mayhem".

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# A [[state]] or situation of great [[confusion]], [[disorder]], [[trouble]] or [[destruction]]; [[chaos]].
#: ''What if the legendary hero Robin Hood had been born into the '''mayhem''' of the 20th century ?''
#: ''In all the '''mayhem''', some children were separated from their partners.''
#: ''She waded into the '''mayhem''', elbowing between taller men to work her way to the front of the crowd.''
#: ''The clowns would dart into the crowd and pull another unsuspecting victim into the '''mayhem''' of the ring''
# Infliction of violent injury on a person or thing.
#: ''The fighting dogs created '''mayhem''' in the flower beds.''
# {{context|legal|lang=en}} The maiming of a person by depriving him of the use of any of his limbs which are necessary for defense or protection.
# {{context|legal|lang=en}} The [[crime]] of [[damaging]] things or [[harming]] people on purpose.

